    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 5 USER]\ne043\tA curator from the showcase listened yesterday.\ne044\tShe loved the ending and said it was the first time the piece felt inevitable.\ne045\tThat word bothered me, but it also means Jonah achieved something.\ne046\tShe can move us to a later program, though she cannot guarantee the same room or promotion.\ne047\tJonah thinks postponing would throw away momentum for a concern the participant may not share.\ne048\tI am starting to wonder whether I am asking the piece to preserve every ambiguity because I am afraid of making an argument.\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne049\tDocumentaries do make arguments through selection, and preserving every hesitation can be another form of distortion.\ne050\tThe curator's response is useful evidence about the ending's force, not evidence that its interpretation is fair.\ne051\tThe later program reduces the deadline's finality but introduces real opportunity costs.\ne052\tYou might distinguish changes required for representational integrity from changes that would make the argument feel more like yours.\ne053\tThat distinction will not be perfectly objective, but trying to articulate it may clarify the disagreement.\n[Turn 6 USER]\ne054\tJonah proposed restoring one moment of hesitation and adding a short scene earlier that shows the participant's obligations in the town.\ne055\tHe would keep the final sequence.\ne056\tI can imagine that working.\ne057\tBut he also wants the credit to read “A film by Jonah Vale and Mira Chen,” even though it is audio, because the showcase uses that language.\ne058\tI want “Reported by Mira Chen, sound and story by Jonah Vale.”\ne059\tHe hears my version as splitting the work into journalism and art.\n[Turn 6 ASSISTANT]\ne060\tHis edit proposal sounds like a substantive attempt to preserve uncertainty without dissolving the ending, though you should judge it in the cut rather than in description.\ne061\tThe credit disagreement is not merely wording; each version tells a story about what the work is.\ne062\tEqual top-line authorship plus a second line naming reporting, editing, and sound may acknowledge collaboration and labor, but only if neither of you experiences the top line as erasing the actual imbalance you are trying to name.",
